1 Introduction
Classroom teaching is a crucial social process that involves extended interactions between teachers and students. Within this context, teacher-student dialogues promote the exchange of knowledge, skills, and ideas, and demonstrate cognitive processes such as questioning, critical thinking, answering, relecting, and receiving feedback. These dialogues serve as a vital medium for efective communication and the development of independent thinking skills among students. Classroom dialogues, characterized by their complexity, present formidable challenges for processing and utilization for traditional machine learning methods. This complexity arises from their inherent conversational nature, the diverse constraints, individual teaching styles, and the diiculty in establishing quantiiable evaluation standards. The processing of such conversational texts demands a high degree of professional knowledge and pedagogical insight to accurately capture contextual continuity, maintain to language standards, and align with educational objectives. In this paper, we explore recent advancements in Artiicial Intelligence for education purposes and focus on utilizing the emerging Large Language Models (LLMs) for improving classroom teaching by generating meaningful and creative dialogues between teachers and students and providing benchmarks for evaluation. Our commitment lies in the development and application of advanced language model technologies, aiming to more efectively process and generalize limited classroom dialogue data from human experts to various topics and subjects. The recent progress in large language models (LLMs), exempliied by the GPT series [8, 46], has signiicantly revolutionized both academic and practical approaches to natural language processing (NLP) tasks. AI-generated content, often abbreviated as AI-generated content (AIGC), has become prevalent in various NLP and Vision tasks, including the creation of conversational dialogues and artistic images [1]. Nevertheless, the application of AIGC for educational purposes remains an under-explored task. This task is particularly critical in primary and high school classrooms in remote areas where qualiied teachers are scarce. While classroom teaching videos and materials are available online, the cost of educating experienced teachers is often prohibitively high. This prompts us to consider whether AIGC can be leveraged to create classroom teaching dialogues, aiming to enhance student education and support teachers simultaneously. In this paper, we explore and verify the potential of employing LLMs to enhance the quality of classroom teaching (CT) and generate AIGC to supplement teaching resources. We focus on two teaching scenarios over two common courses and investigate the feasibility of utilizing AIGC to generate high-quality data for educational purposes. The irst scenario we explore is the automatic completion of a teaching dialogue, abbreviated as łdialogue completion.ž This situation arises from the recognition that the dialogue between teachers and students can occasionally be disrupted due to unforeseen events. For example, technical malfunctions, such as a sudden breakdown of the projector, can impede the low of the dialogue by depriving the teacher of presentation tools. Addressing such disruptions swiftly can be challenging for human teachers. We investigate the application of AIGC to complete missing dialogues, assisting teachers in quickly recovering from a disrupted classroom teaching scenario. In the second scenario, we focus on the transfer of existing syllabi or teaching schemes from expert teachers to new topics, abbreviated as łexpertise transfer.ž This approach leverages the value of teaching schemes crafted by experienced educators. However, it is challenging for inexperienced teachers to quickly acquire the necessary skills and guidance for a new topic based on a speciic teaching dialogue. Consequently, delivering a well-designed and efective lesson can be diicult. We pose the question of whether AIGC can be employed to transfer the teaching style from one subject to another while adhering to the original dialogue plan and maintaining the earnest dialogical styles.
