In this paper, we demonstrate the superior applicability of the proposed Educational LLM framework (ELF) in the ield of classroom teaching. Speciically, based on the annotated cases mentioned above, we identify two crucial and imperative areas of research: 1) Teacher-Student Dialogue Auto-Completion and 2) Cross-discipline Expertise Transfer Learning. In order to scientiically evaluate the performance of ELF in actual educational tasks, we further propose two evaluation criteria: 1) Perplexity-based Statistical Evaluation (PPL) and 2) Human Evaluation with Questionnaires (HEQ). By organically integrating LLMs and AIGCs into classroom teaching, we aim to address the opportunities and challenges brought by this innovative approach. We will elaborate on these scenarios in Section 3. We irst propose a uniied framework - Educational LLM Framework (ELF) to present our proposed framework holistically. Then in Section 4, we discuss each classroom teaching task and the steps required to solve them in detail. Finally, in Section 5.3, we evaluate the quality of the content generated using two criteria. We summarize our contributions in this paper as follows.
(1) We consider a critical and under-explored topic in AIGC For Education and how to incorporate the capable large language models into classroom teaching properly and efectively. (2) We self-collected teaching datasets of topics including literature and mathematics from publicly available videos of expert teachers’ lectures, which we would like to make public to boost future research. (3) We propose a uniied framework Educational LLM Framework (ELF) which tackles the two most applicable teaching scenarios including dialogue completion and expertise transfer learning. (4) We propose two novel methods - Context Learning (CL) and Dynamic Role Setting (DyRS) - for ofering a comprehensive solution suitable for classical teaching scenarios. CL enhances the LLM’s perception and comprehension of context by better learning contextual representations, and DyRS injects identity representations into LLM to simulate dynamic roles’ linguistic styles and professional knowledge. (5) We identify two practical methods for comprehensively assessing the quality of generated content: the Perplexity-based statistical evaluation (PPL) and the Human Evaluation with Questionnaires (HEQ). (6) Our experimental results verify that our enhanced AI content generation methodology for classroom teaching approaches the quality of human expert-annotated data, in certain tasks or datasets, even surpasses it. This outcome suggests a promising direction for future research.
2 Related Work
Teacher-student dialogue is a crucial medium for interaction in classroom teaching and has been extensively studied in the pedagogical ield [36, 38]. Productive classroom dialogue fosters deeper understanding, critical thinking and academic achievement [28]. However, many teachers often lack the awareness and strategies necessary to efectively promote such dialogue [6]. Given these potential beneits and practical challenges, it is essential to provide templates for efective conversations. Therefore, we aim to enhance classroom teaching by generating productive dialogues through the integration of LLMs. Dialogue assessment. Numerous pedagogical studies have sought to evaluate classroom dialogue [13], especially for mathematics and science [4, 5, 30]. Direct approaches include collecting student feedback for detailed evaluations [50, 54], or employing machine learning methods for predictive analysis [56, 57]. Researches [40] have explored various aspects, such as students’ thinking processes from diverse viewpoints, dialogic practices aimed at speciic outcomes [25], group interaction performance [30], and performance scoring [29]. Recent studies [58] have involved transcribing and systematically coding classroom dialogue, using lag sequential pattern mining technique to examine dialogic contributions and identify patterns in exchanges. This approach has facilitated an examination of how dialogue structure and content evolve [39, 40]. A study [55] showed that the efectiveness of coding frameworks in analyzing classroom dialogue. Given the complexity of dialogic interaction in classroom settings, study [10] introduced sequential pattern mining techniques to capture
