Large Language Models (LLMs) are advanced mathematical models that predict the statistical distribution of words in extensive corpora of human-generated text. A prominent example is ChatGPT [73], which showcases impressive conversational abilities with humans. LLMs are capable of performing various tasks based solely on natural language descriptions, eliminating the need for task-speciic training [60]. Nowadays, LLMs signiicantly impact the AI community, particularly in NLP, where they are increasingly used as general-purpose language task solvers. The ield of LLMs has seen signiicant advancements in enhancing the quality of response, particularly through techniques such as Chain-of-Thought (CoT) [66], Tree-of-thought (ToT) [70] techniques and Self-consistency [64]. Additionally, other notable eforts have further improved LLM performance. For instance, Federated-LLM [33] introduces intermediate steps that help LLMs explore diferent reasoning paths before making decisions. The Self-Reine approach [37] enhances model outputs by using iterative feedback, showing that even top models can beneit from simple, test-time methods. Fine-tune-CoT [26] employs a large teacher model to generate samples that ine-tune a smaller model, often making it more efective than the teacher. Learn-Babble-Prune [7] boosts the reliability of outputs by rejecting responses without strong evidence, ensuring answers are grounded in the model’s learned knowledge. Recent surveys [9, 68, 73] have extensively discussed the advancements in improving LLMs, ofering a thorough overview of various techniques and their strengths, limitations, and future potential. Rather than proposing general methods for enhancing LLM prompting or training, our focus is on improving LLMs in classroom teaching through speciic techniques like context learning and dynamic role setting. Large Language Models (LLMs) in Education. A survey [63] highlights the emergence of LLMs in various education applications, such as study assisting, teaching assisting and adaptive learning, which can be categorized based on its users’ role, showing that the rapid development of LLMs has revolutionized education. Curriculum Instruction Tuning [22] utilizes a teacher LLM to design a curriculum for student LLM training, illustrating the potential applications of LLMs in educational contexts. Abdelghani et al. [2] explore LLMs as pedagogical agents to foster curiosity-driven learning, indicating that LLMs can signiicantly enhance the expression of curiosity among learners. Dijkstra [15] further demonstrates the utility of LLMs in creating interactive educational materials to improve student learning outcomes and engagement. From the educators’ perspective, Kasneci et al. [31] investigate LLMs’s role in automating student answers assessment, providing adaptive feedback and generating instructional content. Moore et al. [43] further suggest that when LLMs are ine-tuned to speciic domains, they can be powerful tools for teachers in evaluating the quality and pedagogical value of student responses. Additionally, recent discussions [51, 65, 71] have focused on the automatic generation of mathematical word problems, highlighting the potential of LLMs to understand equations and contextualize them appropriately. Collectively, these studies underscore the promise of leveraging LLMs to generate long-term content for classroom education, thereby enhancing the teaching and learning experience. AI-Generated Content (AIGC) in Education. In recent years, the development of AI-Generated Content (AIGC) has attracted signiicant attention, especially with the emergence of GPT series [45, 46], DALL-E-2 [20] and DreamFusion [49]. Large-scale models become increasingly important in AIGC and can provide better intent extraction and improved generation results. Moreover, with the continuous iteration of models and methods, the distribution that models can learn becomes more comprehensive and closer to reality, thus generating more realistic and higher-quality content [9, 16]. Research [67] indicates that AIGC signiicantly enhances personalized and dynamic learning experiences, thereby improving teaching quality and eiciency. AIGC technology fosters content innovation in education by integrating real-time data, ofering new perspectives, tools and creating a more interactive and personalized
