In this section, we delve into the challenges associated with efectively generating instructional conversations using existing Large Language Models (LLMs). To address these challenges, we introduce a uniied framework, as illustrated in Fig. 1, that harnesses LLMs for generating pedagogical content under two fundamental pedagogical scenarios: Dialog Completion and Expertise Transfer Learning. Moreover, our exploration spans two distinct subjects - literature and mathematics. We will irst introduce our self-collected datasets in Section 3.1, and provide an overview of our techniques for improving and assessing the generated contents in Section 3.2 and Section 3.3.
3.1 Overview of our self-collected classroom teaching datasets
With the rapid advancement of technology and the lourishing of education, teachers typically record their lessons with the assistance of an Intelligent Recording and Broadcasting (IRB) system, which provides high-deinition audio and video recordings. After obtaining their students’ consent, these recordings are uploaded to the łNational Education Resources Public Service Platformž1, a publicly accessible and free resource. This platform ofers authentic classroom dialogue data, featuring lectures by master teachers. Leveraging this resource, we transcribed high-quality audio collected through the IRB system into text, creating an experimental dataset. This access to genuine classroom interactions provides a robust foundation for AI in education research, supported by a primary corpus of classroom teaching interactions. Considering the enormity of the dataset, we randomly sampled diferent samples of classroom dialogues to ensure the representativeness of the experiment. We selected 20 courses each for the topics of Chinese Literature and Mathematics, with each course lasting 40 minutes, as our experimental data. These courses span a broad range of student groups from grades one to nine and encompass diverse teaching topics. These two subjects were selected due to their representative nature, covering the basic courses of Arts and Sciences respectively, and their compulsory status in the education curriculum, thus providing valuable reference data. The dialogue scenes focus on whole-class teaching, with typical class sizes of around 40 students, as mandated by the Ministry of Education. Previous studies [27, 41] have shown that small-group dialogue is often unproductive due to students’ lack of understanding of group discussion purposes and execution. Group work is rarely structured to facilitate rich exchanges of opinion, therefore we focus on whole-class dialogue. Table 1 is a detailed description of the two datasets, containing Sub-topics, Course Distribution, Dial