When dealing with vast and complex text data, especially in dialogue scenarios, existing LLMs often encounter challenges related to memory decay, which impede their ability to efectively capture global information in long texts. Moreover, as LLMs generate text based on a wide range of potential responses, variability in the style of the generated content of arises. These limitation can result in the omission of key information and insuicient attention to details, ultimately afects the quality of output. To address this issue, we innovatively designed the łContext Learningž (CL) module, which includes two methods: In-Context-Guidance (ICG) and Cross-Context-Guidance (CCG). In-Context-Guidance is a methodology widely used to enhance the performance of LLMs by leveraging contextual information to guide their actions and decisions. For example, GraphRAG [18] summarizes all documents to facilitate mapping for retrieval, enabling the model to assess and utilize relevant context eiciently. Similarity, Relexion [52] iteratively generates actions and responses, summarizing feedback into its memory to relect and generate new actions. The REFLECT framework [34] guides the model by summarizing a robot’s current experiences and failure explanations, which helps in correcting its current plan. In our approach, ICG enhances the model’s ability to capture the overall context and key information of the text through a global perception mechanism. This reduces content forgetting, improves performance in processing long texts, and intelligently ilters redundant details to ensure more accurate generated content. Cross-Context-Guidance was initially applied in the ield of vision, as demonstrated by Image Style Transfer using convolutional networks [23], which maps the style of one image onto another with completely diferent content. In Natural Language Processing (NLP), a widely adopted cross-context learning technique is Chain of Thought (CoT) [66], which provides an output template for the LLM to follow. In our approach, CCG enhances the model’s ability to grasp the nuances of generation style by cross-learning from and aligning with given templates. This innovative design signiicantly improves the accuracy and stylistic consistency of LLMs when handling complex text tasks. Without additional prompts, LLMs tend to generate general content lacking deeper professional-level thinking. Therefore, to enhance LLMs’ ability to generate high-quality content in the ield of education, we propose the addition of the Dynamic Role Setting module. This role could be illed by a variety of professionals, such as English literature, mathematics, or physics teachers accordingly. This explicit setting of role player provides speciic context for the generated content. The purpose of this module is to improve engagement and personalization by setting speciic role characteristics and requirements when generating teaching content. This enhances the personalization and adaptability of the generated content, allowing the model to produce more targeted output based on the needs and role characteristics of the target audience. Simultaneously, this design can also tap into LLMs’ thinking on speciic domains to some extent, enabling them to generate more professional and normative expressions. Such a module can make LLMs generate more thoughtful and professional content in the ield of education, improving teaching quality and providing users with richer and more valuable educational resources.
3.3 Generate content quality assessment
To comprehensively and accurately evaluate the practical efectiveness of our proposed method in improving the quality of content generated by LLMs, we have adopted two complementary assessment approaches: statistical evaluation based on perplexity (PPL) and human evaluation based on questionnaires (HEQ). The combination of these two methods enables in-depth and detailed analysis of the quality of generated text from both quantitative statistical indicators and human perception perspectives. As a standard evaluation metric in the ield of natural language processing, perplexity is widely used to measure the degree of it between a language model and test data. A lower perplexity value typically indicates stronger predictive capabilities of the model for text data, indirectly relecting higher quality of generated content. We chose to use perplexity as one of our evaluation metrics precisely because it provides objective and quantiiable results, facilitating comparisons and analyses of diferences between diferent methods. In this experiment, we employed a perplexity calculation method based on GPT-2 to assess the luency and consistency of the text generated by the model. This approach allows us to obtain a speciic numerical result, enabling more intuitive judgments about whether the quality of the model’s generated content has improved. However, while statistical evaluation based on perplexity can provide quantitative metrics for text quality, it still struggles to fully capture human perception of text complexity. To address this limitation, we further proposed a human evaluation method based on questionnaires. Through designing comprehensive questionnaires, we collect evaluators’ feedback on the generated content from two key dimensions: semantic analysis and educational aspects. The semantic analysis dimension includes evaluations of the generated content’s language smoothness, clarity of expression, and contextual consistency. And the educational dimension includes the evaluation of factual accuracy, richness of instructional strategies, and rationality of
