4.1.2 Experimental Setup. Our research shows that traditional zero-shot learning methods, while capable of some efectiveness, generally fail to generate dialogues of satisfactory quality when applied to the task of completing classroom dialogues. Such methods may only be able to achieve basic contiguity with the preceding context but perform poorly in terms of coherence with subsequent sections. In some cases, the model demonstrates an insuicient understanding of the task itself and directly continues the following dialogue content instead of completing the omitted portion as intended. To address these challenges, we propose an approach called łIn-Context Guidance (ICG)ž. Speciically, we guide the LLM to summarize the classroom dialogue containing missing content and distill it into ideas for classroom design. This allows the model to grasp the classroom dialogue containing omissions from a holistic perspective. Experimental results show that when the LLM summarizes and distills out ideas for classroom design, it will spontaneously ill in the missing fragment content during the process of generating ideas for classroom design, indirectly self-prompting to indicate the general direction for completing the dialogue. This approach ensures not only coherence within the dialogue context but also a close match with the teacher’s overall teaching design concept. To further optimize the generation of dialogue content, we introduce a role assignment strategy to fully leverage the LLM’s potential. The speciic implementation process of our method is as follows: Step 1: Input. Provide the classroom dialogue containing omitted content as input. Step 2: Global Understanding. Use In-Context Guidance (ICG) to guide the LLM to summarize the input classroom dialogue containing gaps and distill the core ideas for classroom design. This aims to give the LLM a global understanding of the dialogue while removing redundant detail information. Step 3: Dynamic Role Assignment. To obtain more professional and educationally valid output, we assign the LLM a speciic role, such as łmaster teacherž. By providing the prompt łAssume you are a master teacherž, we expect the model to generate more apt and professional teaching content from this role’s perspective. Step 4: Task Explanation. Provide the complete teaching plan and identiication of the incomplete fragment (such as name or number) to clearly instruct the LLM to complete the task of illing in the missing piece. This helps ensure the model has a clear understanding of the speciic task to be completed. Step 5: Generation. Based on the previous steps and guidance, we have the LLM generate the completed dialogue content. We conducted a comparative study of this method versus traditional łzero-shotž methods. In łzero-shotž methods, we directly provide the classroom dialogue containing gaps to the LLM and prompt it to generate the missing middle portion. Through in-depth analysis of the text generated by the LLM under the two methods, we found that our proposed method signiicantly outperforms łzero-shotž methods in terms of accuracy, consistency of style and richness of content. The speciic comparative results and in-depth analysis will be discussed in detail in Section 5.3.
