4.2 Expertise Transfer Learning
4.2.1 Task Description. It is widely known that improving teachers’ teaching abilities often requires them to continuously absorb and practice successful teaching methods, thereby honing teaching skills [21, 74]. However, excellence in teaching talent is not distributed evenly, with remote areas particularly lacking. At the same time, masters’ classroom designs are usually viewed as benchmarks for teaching quality due to their uniqueness and eiciency. Their distinctive teaching philosophies and styles cannot only spark students’ curiosity but also deepen students’ understanding and application of knowledge. However, master teachers are inevitably limited in number, meaning most students cannot directly experience the charm of master classrooms. Therefore, how to quickly and accurately learn and inherit the teaching styles of these excellent education practitioners has become an urgent problem in current education. To address this issue, we conceived the scenario of łExpertise Transfer Learningž. The core is to utilize the powerful understanding capabilities of LLM to deeply analyze the essence of existing master teachers’ classroom designs and comprehensively and organically extend them to a new topic while maintaining the original teaching wisdom, teaching methods and coherent teaching structure. This educational concept is proposed based on a deep understanding of the thirst for quality educational resources and scientiic judgment of the transferability of teaching styles. The excellence of master teachers’ classroom designs lies in their profound grasp of knowledge points, precise guidance of student thinking, and skillful regulation of the classroom atmosphere.
