4.2.2 Experimental Setup. In previous research [35], we observed that while the results of zero-shot methods appear acceptable on the surface in the Expertise Transfer Learning task, there are still many issues such as unreasonable time allocation, overly generic teaching content lacking focus, and irregular expressions. To address these issues, we proposed a new method called łCross-Context-Guidance (CCG)ž. The core idea is to irst utilize existing master design ideas as templates, and fed as prompt preixes into LLM. In our experiment, given that there are currently many lightweight and adaptive retrieval methods [47], we can easily provide an expert template for each discipline to better it the characteristics of each discipline by retrieving from the database. Experimental results showed that the template-based method produced more formally expressed content with a more reasonable time allocation and prominent teaching focus, achieving our expected efects to some degree. To further optimize the quality and professionalism of the
