5.2 Human Evaluation with uestionnaires (HEQ)
Besides using PPL based on statistical language modeling, we also conduct qualitative evaluation through human assessment of the generated content quality. To ensure comprehensive evaluation, we design a ive-point Likert scoring survey. Speciically, we invited 10 human judges and asked them to give a 1-5 score, with higher score indicating better quality, for each question in the questionnaires. In order to collect high-quality feedback, we selected our human judges from a pool of educators and students specializing in educational ields in our normal school. These participants have undergone formal training in pedagogy and possess a strong understanding of dialogue quality in an educational context. Their expertise allows them to evaluate the AI-generated content efectively, ensuring that the feedback collected is both relevant and informed. This survey covers two main aspects: 1) Semantic Analysis and 2) Educational Signiicance, and both of them are split into three speciied points. We will explain the question settings in all questionnaires in detail below. GRUEN [75] is an excellent tool for evaluating generated text, which focuses on content such as grammaticality, nonredundancy, focus, structure, and coherence, suggesting to some extent that language luency is an important metric for evaluating the quality of text generated. Coherence is also a crucial factor in evaluating text, and research [61] emphasizes that the context must meet coherence criteria to efectively convey the message. Based on the theoretical knowledge mentioned above, we ask the human judges to rate the generated content based on criteria covering 1) Language Smoothness, 2) Clarity of Expression, and 3) Contextual Consistency.
• Language Smoothness assesses grammatical correctness and natural expression. • Clarity of Expression gauges the ease with which readers or listeners can understand the content. • Contextual Consistency refers to the coherence and alignment of relevant information or behavior within a speciic context.
Additionally, the metric of usefulness mentioned in research [19] subjectively evaluates the practical application of the question in classroom teaching, encompassing its potential use in lectures, assignments, tests, etc. Thus, we designed the educational analysis covering 1) Factual Accuracy, 2) Richness of Instructional Strategies, and 3) Rationality of Instructional Organization to evaluate the usefulness of the generated contents.
• Factual Accuracy relects whether the teaching content strictly adheres to the accuracy and scientiic nature of academic knowledge, and whether the knowledge conveyed is consistent with the knowledge system of the discipline. • Richness of Instructional Strategies relects whether teachers can lexibly apply a variety of teaching methods and means according to students’ learning needs and characteristics, such as lecturing, demonstration, discussion, case analysis, role playing and other forms. • Rationality of Instructional Organization relects the overall planning and design of the teacher’s classroom teaching process, which requires teachers to reasonably arrange the sequence and progress of teaching content according to students’ cognitive laws and the requirements of teaching objectives, and to ensure that each teaching link is closely connected and orderly.
By providing the ive-point scoring based on our designed criteria, we provide a comprehensive assessment of the content generation quality from human judges. The use of human evaluation methods also enhances the objectivity and reliability of the assessment, thereby increasing the credibility of the research.
