6 Conclusion and Future Challenges
In this paper, we proposed a uniied framework that tackles the application of powerful large artiicial intelligence models in classical classroom teaching scenarios, making it one of the irst eforts to tailor AI-generated content for pedagogical purposes. We focus on two typical use cases of AIGC including 1) dialogue auto-completion and 2) expertise transfer learning. We evaluate the generated contents thoroughly and comprehensively with two designed evaluation methods, including human judgment with designed questionnaires as well as coherence scoring with LLMs. For the former, we design questionnaires based on semantic relations and relevance to the educational ield to obtain lexible evaluation results from humans. For the latter, we encode LLM to obtain coherence and perplexity scores of generated sentences from likelihood logarithmic scores, so that the results can be judged based on the same criteria, which helps objectively evaluate the quality of generated content. Ultimately, we hope that this paper and our uniied Educational LLM Framework (ELF) will have a constructive impact in helping more AI and pedagogical researchers understand and push the frontiers of emerging concepts in LLMs and AIGC. We focus on leveraging a limited amount of expert-annotated data to release the high capacity of the LLMs for educational purposes and applications. Our proposed methodologies include guiding the LLMs with demonstrations, learning contextual representation, dynamic role setting and additional disclaimers. We demonstrate that our methods can provide high-quality classroom teaching dialogues as well as expert instructional design ideas in the disciplines of literature and mathematics. Through extensive evaluations with both classical NLP metric PPL and human evaluation in terms of language smoothness, clarity of expression, and contextual consistency, we conirm the ability of ELF to improve the quality of educational data generation, which in turn provides strong guidance for more efective application of LLMs and AIGC. The future challenge lies in how to generate rich multimedia content such as igures, videos, and soundtracks. This capability is crucial to improving interactivity and engagement in educational materials. By advancing the ability of LLMs to manage and integrate various media types, we can ofer more dynamic and comprehensive learning experiences. Recent studies [17, 62] proposing methods to handle data from multi-annotated datasets using collaborative learning and local
